      "dom_id": "question-26744",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x+2)-2?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y=f(x+2)-2$  is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the *left* and $2$ units down. So the $y$-coordinate decreases by $2$ and the $x$-coordinate decreases by $2.$\n\nWe know that the point $(1,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(1-2,-1-2)=(-1,-3)$ must lie on $y=f(x+2)-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245391/assets/89f839666dc5471bfe43064d01c9c4f5c60a9ac4bfc8abe8cf83ac6d6592bf42.png)",
